<article class="env-lemma" id="090Q">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/090Q">Lemma <span data-tag="090Q">10.50.15</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a ring. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$A$ is a valuation ring, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$A$ is a local domain and every finitely generated ideal of $A$ is principal. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume $A$ is a valuation ring and let $f_1, \ldots , f_ n \in A$. Choose $i$ such that $v(f_ i)$ is minimal among $v(f_ j)$. Then $(f_ i) = (f_1, \ldots , f_ n)$. Conversely, assume $A$ is a local domain and every finitely generated ideal of $A$ is principal. Pick $f, g \in A$ and write $(f, g) = (h)$. Then $f = ah$ and $g = bh$ and $h = cf + dg$ for some $a, b, c, d \in A$. Thus $ac + bd = 1$ and we see that either $a$ or $b$ is a unit, i.e., either $g/f$ or $f/g$ is an element of $A$. This shows $A$ is a valuation ring by Lemma <a href="/tag/052K" data-tag="052K">10.50.5</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>